{"request": {"input_type": "document", "model": "voyage-4", "text": "The account manager describes how writing to existing customers differs from writing to prospects: a one-business-day reply rule to avoid churn, tailoring messages to the economic buyer with cost and time-saved framing, and putting a ten percent discount for two-year renewals directly in the renewal email, which they say sales disagrees with. The interviewer then asks what customers most often ask.\n\nInterviewer: You handle existing customers. How is writing to them different from writing to prospects?\n\nAccount manager: The relationship is already there, so I am more direct. But the first rule I follow is speed. I reply to every customer email within one business day, even if the answer is \"I am checking and will come back on Thursday\". Silence is what makes people churn.\n\nInterviewer: Do you adapt the message to who is reading?\n\nAccount manager: A lot. When I write to the person who signs the contract, the economic buyer, I lead with cost of ownership and the time their team saves, not with features. They do not care about a new chart type. They care that the report that took two days now takes an hour.\n\nInterviewer: And renewals?\n\nAccount manager: Renewals are where I disagree a bit with sales. For mid-sized customers, I put a ten percent renewal discount directly in the renewal email when they renew for two years. It closes faster, and it is our standard offer, so I do not see the risk. Sales prefers to keep it on the phone, but for renewals writing it down saves everyone a meeting.\n\nInterviewer: What do customers ask you most?"}, "vector": [0.016382837668061256, -0.003832830348983407, 0.011409524828195572, -0.019594132900238037, 0.04237719625234604, -0.017180413007736206, -0.04089587181806564, -0.009732636623084545, -0.016359925270080566, 0.03861432522535324, -3.3369113225489855e-05, 0.07556300610303879, -0.013184202834963799, 0.030278030782938004, -0.032722122967243195, 0.035210